chapters=[
('1  Introduction','SmartEdu is a browser-based college portal that consolidates examination, coursework, attendance and learning support. It separates tasks by user role and applies academic scope to content. The repository implements the product as a Django project with independent applications for accounts, exams, student services, teacher services and supporting workflows.',[
('Background and institutional context','Colleges often coordinate assessment, attendance, teaching resources and student communication through disconnected tools. This increases repeated data entry and makes it harder to follow a learner’s activity over time. A shared portal can provide consistent identity, academic scope and workflow records while retaining distinct responsibilities for administrators, teachers and students.'),('Problem statement','The project targets a practical coordination problem: academic work must reach the intended students, assessments need a traceable lifecycle, and teachers need timely evidence for review. A portal must also avoid presenting students with work outside their programme or semester and must let administrators maintain the underlying institutional records.'),('Project purpose','The purpose is to implement a role-oriented academic portal that combines online exams, assignments, attendance, study materials, performance summaries and notifications. The codebase emphasizes workflows and data relationships rather than claiming institutional scale or validated learning gains.'),('Project objectives','The objectives are to manage role-specific accounts; author and conduct timed exams; score objective and written responses; assign and review coursework; record attendance; share study resources; support missed-exam requests; summarize performance; and notify students or guardians where configured.'),('Scope of the application','The supplied project covers a Django web application, its database models, HTML templates, static assets, AI evaluation utilities and demo data. It includes account administration, teacher and student portals, attendance, re-attempts, study material, analytics and notification logging. Institutional single sign-on and a production hosting platform are outside the supplied scope.'),('Stakeholders','Primary users are administrators, teachers and students. Guardians receive email updates where student records and SMTP configuration allow. Project guides, support staff and future maintainers are secondary stakeholders who need clear requirements, access boundaries, deployment instructions and known limitations.'),('Operating assumptions','The application assumes that administrators enter valid users, departments and student semesters; teachers have an assigned department; and students use supported browsers. Webcam access requires a secure browser context or localhost. Real email delivery requires correctly configured SMTP credentials.'),('Constraints and boundaries','The project uses SQLite by default and is presented as a development-friendly college application. Its assessment algorithms are heuristic and its proctoring checks are browser-based. No supplied source establishes production availability, formal security certification, large-scale load capacity or efficacy in detecting cheating.'),('Report organization','The report moves from problem and requirements to system design, feature workflows, verification and operations. Appendices translate the design into practical checklists and test scenarios. The sequence is intended to help an examiner trace a requirement through the relevant implementation and understand what evidence is still required.')]),
('2  Feasibility and planning','This chapter frames feasibility as a technical and organizational assessment of the repository, not as a completed institutional business case. Django, SQLite and bundled frontend assets reduce setup complexity for a demonstration. Production use would require additional decisions about hosting, privacy, backups, email delivery and operational support.',[
('Technical feasibility','The source uses a conventional Django structure with URL routing, models, forms, views, templates and migrations. A local Python environment can run the project with SQLite. Optional OpenCV support is isolated for webcam face checks, while the application otherwise runs without that package according to the repository documentation.'),('Operational feasibility','The role model aligns with familiar college responsibilities: administrators manage accounts, teachers create academic work and students complete it. Adoption would still require staff training, account provisioning, data quality procedures, support ownership and a decision on how the portal fits existing academic policies.'),('Economic feasibility','The repository relies on open-source frameworks and does not specify a licensing fee for its core stack. The total cost of operation cannot be inferred from source code: hosting, backups, domain names, monitoring, email service, support time and security review remain deployment-specific.'),('Schedule and project phases','A reasonable academic development plan separates discovery, requirements, implementation, integration, verification, documentation and demonstration. The repository shows integrated features but does not provide a project schedule or time logs. Students should insert their actual milestone dates and contribution records.'),('Risk assessment approach','Risks should be assessed by likelihood, impact, detection and mitigation. Relevant areas include unauthorized access, incorrect academic scoping, stale or lost data, email misconfiguration, unfair automated scoring, browser compatibility and overloaded administration. The project report treats these as engineering concerns rather than claiming they have been eliminated.'),('Risk register: account and data integrity','Weak passwords, shared accounts or mistaken role assignment can expose student records. Mitigations include secure password policy, least-privilege administration, review of account changes and audit records. The development secret key and DEBUG defaults must not be carried unchanged into production.'),('Risk register: assessment fairness','Heuristic scoring may misjudge a valid written response, and browser signals may produce false alarms. The system provides teacher review paths, but the institution must define appeal and override procedures. Published scoring rules and transparent feedback are important for student trust.'),('Risk register: operations and availability','A local SQLite file is convenient but requires deliberate backup and restore procedures. Media uploads and database records must be backed up consistently. Production deployment should use HTTPS, a controlled host list, monitored services and a tested recovery plan.'),('Feasibility conclusion','The codebase is feasible as a local academic demonstration and a foundation for controlled piloting. A real college rollout depends on additional operational controls, institutional acceptance, privacy review, deployment engineering and measured evaluation. Feasibility should be revisited with the target institution’s requirements.')]),
('3  Requirements specification','Requirements in this report describe observable system behavior. Functional requirements summarize implemented workflows from the repository; quality requirements identify expectations that should be measured during institutional acceptance. The implementation has demo data and automated tests, but test execution results are not asserted here.',[
('Requirement method and traceability','Each requirement should have an identifier, rationale, actor, preconditions, main flow, exception paths and acceptance evidence. A traceability matrix maps requirements to Django applications, routes, models and verification scenarios. The repository’s tests are a useful starting point but do not replace user acceptance criteria.'),('Functional requirements: accounts','The system shall authenticate users and distinguish administrator, teacher and student roles. Administrators shall register, edit, deactivate or delete student and teacher records within the portal’s supported flows. Student registration includes academic placement and guardian details.'),('Functional requirements: academic scope','Academic content shall be associated with a department and semester. Student access shall match both values. Teacher activities shall follow the department assigned to that teacher, with semester selection for the work they create or review. Semester validation shall respect the four-semester MBA/MCA limit.'),('Functional requirements: examinations','Teachers shall create exams, schedule opening and closing times, add MCQ or written questions, publish exams and review submissions. Students shall see eligible published exams, follow instructions, submit answers and view available results. Teachers shall be able to review or adjust marks within supported controls.'),('Functional requirements: assignments and resources','Teachers shall create assignments with topic, optional instructions and due date; students shall submit permitted files; and teachers shall review with a rating and feedback. Teachers shall share a file or link as study material for a department and semester.'),('Functional requirements: attendance and communication','Teachers shall record attendance for a date and semester. Students shall view their own attendance history. The application shall create relevant notifications and log guardian emails when the configured workflow sends them.'),('Functional requirements: missed exams and analytics','Eligible students shall submit a missed-exam reason and proof according to validation rules. The exam teacher shall approve with a deadline or reject with a comment. Performance views shall summarize exams and assignment ratings and identify weak topics according to configured logic.'),('Non-functional requirements','The interface should be usable in current desktop browsers, protect authenticated routes, validate input on the server, provide understandable errors, and keep uploaded files access-controlled. Maintainability should be supported through modular Django apps. Performance and capacity targets must be defined and tested for the intended deployment.'),('Acceptance criteria and exclusions','Acceptance should confirm role access, academic scoping, exam lifecycle, uploads, attendance, notification handling and deployment configuration. It should include negative tests and a review of privacy handling. Accessibility conformance, integration with college SIS systems and measured learning outcomes are not established by the supplied code.')]),
('4  System analysis and use cases','System analysis decomposes SmartEdu by user goals, inputs, outputs, constraints and failure paths. The project is a server-rendered Django application: browser requests reach URL patterns, views apply role and scope checks, models persist records, and templates render the response.',[
('System context','The application boundary includes account management, academic workflows, scoring utilities, notifications and stored uploads. External dependencies include the user’s browser, optional webcam and microphone, SMTP server for real guardian delivery, and the host environment. College identity systems and student information systems are not integrated in the supplied repository.'),('Actors and goals','Administrators maintain users and academic structure; teachers create and assess work; students complete work and review outcomes; guardians receive email information. Each actor has different permissions. The application’s role checks and object ownership checks enforce these distinctions at request time.'),('Use case: register a student','An administrator enters identity, contact, department, semester, roll number and guardian details. The server validates required fields and academic pair validity, then creates the user and profile. Alternate flows include duplicate roll number, invalid semester, missing guardian data or an existing username.'),('Use case: publish an exam','A teacher creates a draft, adds questions, validates the schedule and publishes it. Publication triggers notifications for eligible students matching department and semester. Exceptions include missing questions, invalid dates, duplicate actions or an exam already attempted by students.'),('Use case: take and submit an exam','A student opens an eligible exam during its allowed period, reads instructions, answers questions and submits. The server stores answer records and evaluates supported question types. The lifecycle needs handling for timeout, refresh, disconnected browser, camera denial and repeated submissions.'),('Use case: request a re-attempt','A student who missed an exam may send one request with a reason and proof. The exam’s teacher reviews it and either rejects with a comment or grants an individual deadline. The authorization boundary protects proof files from unrelated users.'),('Use case: mark attendance','The teacher selects a date and semester within the assigned department, marks student statuses and saves. The server updates records, creates absent notifications and attempts guardian email delivery. Duplicate email behavior is intended to avoid repeated messages for the same status and date.'),('Use case: submit and review an assignment','The student uploads a permitted file and optional note. The teacher opens the assignment, inspects submissions and records a rating and review. Once reviewed, the student’s submission is locked against replacement, keeping the feedback and assessed file aligned.'),('Exception handling and user feedback','Validation errors, permission failures, missing files and incorrect time windows should produce clear outcomes without exposing internal details. Django messages are used for workflow feedback. A production review should also inspect logging, error pages and support procedures.')]),
('5  Architecture and design','SmartEdu follows Django’s Model–View–Template structure. Each app owns a domain area, while shared account helpers provide identity and department/semester rules. The architecture allows the exam, attendance and assignment workflows to evolve independently while using the shared user model.',[
('Architectural style','The application is a modular monolith. A single Django project configures installed apps, middleware, templates, database and URLs. Views coordinate validation and persistence; forms encapsulate input handling; models define stored entities; templates render role-specific pages.'),('Project-level configuration','The config package contains settings, root URL patterns, ASGI and WSGI entry points. Settings install the Django framework and project apps, configure SQLite, authentication, template processors, media handling and email behavior. Environment variables override selected deployment values.'),('Application boundaries','Accounts owns the custom user and profiles; teacher and student apps serve role-specific pages; exams owns exam data and attempt behavior; assignments and materials manage coursework and resources; attendance stores status; notifications handle messages and guardian email logs; performance and proctoring provide analysis and event records.'),('Request lifecycle','A browser request is routed by config.urls to an app URL configuration. Authentication middleware identifies the session user. A decorator or view checks role, validates parameters or a form, queries models through the ORM, and renders a template or redirects after a state change.'),('Shared helpers and domain rules','The department helper defines the fixed department list, semester counts, student scoping and teacher department scope. Centralizing these rules reduces inconsistent validation. The server remains authoritative even when JavaScript updates semester options in the browser.'),('Template and static asset design','Templates extend a common base layout and include shared field or department/semester fragments. Static assets provide styling and browser-side behavior such as linked semester options. This approach avoids duplicating navigation and common form markup.'),('Background and external services','The supplied code performs workflows within the web process and uses configured Django email backends. Console email is available in development; SMTP is used when configured. No external task queue or separate AI service is specified.'),('Deployment topology','A production topology would include a reverse proxy, HTTPS, a WSGI/ASGI application server, persistent database, protected media storage, static asset serving, SMTP relay, logs and backups. The repository documentation describes local setup, not a production deployment already in place.'),('Design trade-offs','A modular monolith is straightforward for a college project and local installation. SQLite simplifies demos but can constrain concurrent writes and operational recovery. Server-rendered pages reduce frontend complexity, while browser-only proctoring remains limited by device and browser trust boundaries.')]),
('6  Database and data design','The data model centers on a custom User with one-to-one student or teacher profiles. Academic records link to users, exams, assignments and files. Department and semester are stored together on content that is distributed to a cohort so that audience matching is explicit.',[
('Entity relationship overview','User has role-specific profile records. A student profile holds roll number, department, semester and guardian information. A teacher profile holds employee ID, subject and assigned department. Exams own questions and submissions; assignments own submissions; attendance references student and marking teacher.'),('User and profile data','The custom user extends Django’s AbstractUser and adds role, phone, profile image and verification flag. StudentProfile uses a unique roll number and stores academic placement and guardian details. TeacherProfile uses a unique employee ID and stores subject and assigned department.'),('Exam entities','Exam records include title, subject, department, semester, scheduling, duration, status, creator and descriptive fields. Question records hold question type, text, marks, options, correct answer or ideal answer and keywords. ExamSubmission and answer-related entities track attempts and results.'),('Assignment entities','Assignment records describe the topic, subject, academic audience, due date, creator and notes. AssignmentSubmission relates a student to uploaded work and review fields. The model supports late state and review status in the teacher workflow.'),('Attendance and notification entities','Attendance stores student, date, status, department, semester and marking teacher. Notifications store recipient, sender, type, title, message and optional related exam. EmailLog provides a record of guardian communication attempts and outcomes.'),('Performance and support entities','PerformanceReport stores exam-derived summaries; WeakTopic and Recommendation represent areas for revision and resource suggestions. ProctoringLog stores exam event details. ReattemptRequest stores student reason, proof, status, teacher comment and any approved extension deadline.'),('Relationships and deletion behavior','Django foreign keys define ownership and lifecycle behavior. Some relationships cascade when a parent is removed; others preserve records by setting a reference to null. Deleting a person can remove related records and uploaded files, so administrative deletion requires deliberate confirmation.'),('Data integrity rules','Unique usernames, roll numbers and employee IDs prevent duplicate identifiers. Forms and helper methods validate department/semester combinations and file types. Database-level constraints should be reviewed for critical invariants such as one attendance record per student and date.'),('Migration and legacy data','Migrations evolve schema, including the transition from class names to department and semester fields. The upgrade_departments management command maps common older department labels and reports unmatched values for administrative correction.')]),
('7  Security and privacy design','Security depends on correct configuration and request-level authorization. SmartEdu uses Django authentication, CSRF middleware, role decorators and object ownership checks. This chapter describes the controls visible in the repository and identifies deployment work still required.',[
('Authentication and session handling','Django’s authentication middleware associates requests with logged-in users. Account views handle login and logout. Production settings should use a strong secret key, secure cookies, HTTPS and a defined session lifetime appropriate to college policy.'),('Role-based authorization','Teacher, student and administrator decorators restrict entry to role-specific views. Some resources also check object ownership, such as the teacher who created an exam or assignment. Authorization should be checked on every request, including file endpoints and POST actions.'),('Department and semester authorization','Student content queries use exact department and semester matching. Teacher workflows derive the allowed department from TeacherProfile, and teacher-facing filters should not expand that scope based on a submitted query parameter. Server-side enforcement prevents simple UI manipulation.'),('CSRF and request validation','CSRF middleware protects form-based state changes when templates include tokens. Forms validate values and files on the server. Any state-changing operation should use POST and explicit permission checks; security review should inspect all routes for consistency.'),('File upload controls','Assignments, study materials and re-attempt proofs accept configured extensions and size limits. Private file views enforce role or ownership checks before opening a stored file. Production storage should prevent direct public bypass of those checks and scan uploads where policy requires.'),('Password and secret management','Django password hashing is used through its authentication system. Development settings contain insecure defaults and DEBUG is enabled by default in the inspected configuration. A real deployment must supply a random secret, disable DEBUG and store SMTP credentials outside source control.'),('Personal data and minimization','Student and guardian names, emails, attendance and performance are sensitive educational records. The institution should establish purpose, access, retention, correction and deletion rules. Demo accounts and screenshots should never be confused with real student records.'),('Threats and mitigations','Threats include account takeover, insecure object references, privilege escalation, cross-site scripting, CSRF, malicious uploads, data leakage through logs and loss of backups. Mitigations include least privilege, output escaping, validation, protected media, secure deployment and regular review.'),('Privacy and responsible use','Automated scores and proctoring events can influence student outcomes. Students should be informed about data collection, retention and review procedures. Teachers need a way to inspect and correct questionable results; institutional policy governs any consequential use.')]),
('8  Administrator, teacher and student workflows','The portals share one authentication entry but present different navigation and permitted actions according to role. The role boundary is both a usability choice and an authorization requirement: visible navigation should match the server’s actual permissions.',[
('Administrator dashboard and duties','Administrators oversee accounts, departments, announcements, exam records and guardian email logs. The portal supports student and teacher registration, editing and deletion, account activation controls and academic overview. Administrators should validate source data before bulk operations.'),('Teacher dashboard and duties','The teacher dashboard summarizes exams, submissions and performance flags. Teachers create assessments, manage questions, publish work, review submissions, handle re-attempts, share materials, mark attendance and inspect students within their assigned department.'),('Student dashboard and duties','The student dashboard surfaces exams, performance, attendance, assignments, study resources and notifications. The student’s department and semester shape academic visibility. Student routes should show actionable status and avoid exposing another learner’s data.'),('Navigation and shared layout','The common base template displays role-specific links, notifications and account actions. Shared layout consistency helps users find routine tasks. Role checks must still be applied to endpoints even if navigation links are hidden.'),('Teacher department assignment','Administrators assign a department on teacher registration and can change it later. Current teacher activity uses that assigned department as its fixed scope. Teachers choose a semester where the activity supports semester selection, reducing accidental cross-department targeting.'),('Department and semester filters','The filter fragments support department/semester selection in shared admin and teacher pages. Teacher pages should present the department as fixed and expose the semester as the available cohort filter. Administrators retain broad filters for oversight.'),('Account lifecycle','Registration creates identity and profile data. Editing changes profile details and assignment. Deactivation preserves historical records while limiting access; deletion is permanent and may cascade to records and files. Operational policy should prefer the lifecycle appropriate to the institution.'),('Error and empty states','Empty lists should explain why no records appear and what the user can do next. Invalid form values should be attached to fields. Permission errors should not disclose whether a protected object exists to unauthorized users.'),('Accessibility and usability considerations','Forms need clear labels, keyboard navigation, visible errors and sufficient contrast. Timed exams and webcam prompts should provide understandable status. The repository uses Bootstrap components, but accessibility needs testing with keyboard and screen-reader tools.')]),
('9  Examination lifecycle and re-attempts','The exam workflow moves from teacher authoring to student attempt, scoring and review. Exam time windows and student eligibility are enforced by the server-side views and helpers. Re-attempt requests handle a missed exam as a distinct case from a started or cancelled attempt.',[
('Exam creation and scheduling','A teacher enters exam title, subject, audience, description, duration and opening/closing times. The form checks that the close time follows the start and that the department/semester pair is valid. An exam is created as a draft until the teacher completes its questions.'),('Question authoring','The question form supports MCQ and subjective question types, marks, topic labels and type-specific fields. MCQ questions require options and a correct choice. Subjective questions need an ideal answer or keywords for the automatic evaluator.'),('Publishing and student notification','A teacher publishes an exam after adding at least one question. Publication sends notifications to students matching the exam department and semester. A published exam may return to draft only under the application’s restrictions, including whether attempts already exist.'),('Student eligibility and instructions','Student views list eligible published exams and show instructions before the exam begins. Eligibility includes academic scope and time-window rules. A re-attempt extension applies to a particular student and should not widen cohort access.'),('Attempt, timing and answer storage','The exam page supports answer entry and browser-side interaction. The server persists attempts and answers and applies timing checks. Reliability should be tested for refresh, autosave boundaries, clock differences, slow connections and repeated requests.'),('Automatic and teacher scoring','MCQ responses can be scored by key. Written answers receive heuristic evaluation. Teachers can inspect submissions and adjust marks and feedback through review views. The teacher remains responsible for confirming ambiguous or consequential results.'),('Result lifecycle and feedback','After scoring, the system records totals and percentage, flags low performance according to the threshold and may generate performance reports. Students see feedback according to the result views. Guardian email handling is linked to result events where configured.'),('Missed-exam request flow','A student who did not start before closure can submit a reason and proof. The request is associated with the exam and student. The teacher who owns the exam reviews it and chooses approval with an individual deadline or rejection with explanation.'),('Retake and cancellation controls','Teachers can allow a retake for eligible submission cases, such as an incorrectly cancelled attempt, using the supported reset workflow. Deleting submissions affects performance records, so related reporting and notifications should be reviewed for consistency.')]),
('10  AI evaluation and performance analysis','The AI-related project code is a set of bounded scoring and analysis utilities. It combines deterministic answer-key checks with text similarity, keyword contributions and threshold-based summaries. It should be described as assistive evaluation, not as an autonomous authority.',[
('Evaluation objectives','Automated evaluation aims to reduce repetitive marking and provide early feedback. The MCQ path compares selected options with the answer key. The written-answer path uses lexical similarity and configured keywords to estimate alignment with a teacher-provided ideal answer.'),('MCQ scoring','For an MCQ, the selected option is compared with the stored correct option. A matching response earns the question marks; a non-matching response does not. Teacher-authored answer keys are a critical input and should be checked before publishing.'),('Written response normalization','The subjective evaluator prepares text for comparison by normalizing case and token representation. This reduces surface differences but can still miss semantic equivalence, domain-specific notation, spelling variants and valid alternative reasoning.'),('TF–IDF similarity','The written-answer path uses scikit-learn text vectorization and similarity as part of its evaluation. TF–IDF weights terms by their relative frequency and can measure lexical overlap. Similar wording can score well even when reasoning is weak; a correct paraphrase can score poorly.'),('Keyword bonus and score bounds','Configured keywords can add evidence that a response contains expected concepts. The final score should remain bounded by question marks and interpreted with the evaluator’s rules. Keyword selection should be authored carefully to avoid rewarding irrelevant term stuffing.'),('Feedback generation','The score generator associates automated scores and feedback with answer records and can recompute submission totals after changes. Feedback should be specific enough to guide review and should distinguish automated suggestions from teacher-verified marks.'),('Weak topic detection','Performance analysis aggregates question outcomes by topic and detects topics needing revision based on failure patterns. The threshold and aggregation approach shape which topics are surfaced. Topic labels must be consistent for the output to be meaningful.'),('Recommendations and revision resources','Recommendation logic can connect weak topics to revision resources. A useful recommendation should be relevant, accessible and current. The repository’s configured mappings should be reviewed by educators for course fit and link validity.'),('Thresholds and flags','A configurable threshold of 70% is used to flag low scores in the inspected settings. A threshold is a policy choice rather than a universal definition of student ability. Teachers should interpret flags alongside context and the assessment rubric.'),('Evaluation limitations and human oversight','Text matching cannot reliably assess every discipline or reasoning style. The system needs representative answer sets, teacher calibration and an appeal path. Reported performance should never be presented as measured accuracy unless a documented benchmark study has been conducted.')]),
('11  Attendance, assignments and learning materials','These workflows extend SmartEdu beyond exams. They link cohort-level work to students and provide records that teachers can inspect. The data scope and file permissions should remain consistent across each workflow.',[
('Attendance data model and marking','Attendance records identify a student, date, status, department, semester and marking teacher. The teacher interface loads one cohort and date, pre-populates status controls and saves selected values. The server derives the cohort from the teacher’s assigned department.'),('Attendance notifications and guardian messages','An absent status can create an in-app notification and trigger a guardian email attempt. Email logs provide operational visibility. Duplicate-send rules reduce repeated notices for the same event. A school policy should define corrections and who can amend past attendance.'),('Attendance history and reporting','Students see their own attendance history, while teachers can review records they marked and student summaries. Attendance percentages are derived from recorded status counts. The interpretation of late status and excused absences should align with institutional policy.'),('Assignment creation','Teachers create a topic-based assignment with subject, department, semester, optional note and optional due date. Student recipients are determined by exact academic scope and receive notification. The teacher can view submissions and review status.'),('Student assignment submission','Students upload supported file formats within the configured size limit and may add a note. A submission can be replaced before review. The system marks submissions after the deadline as late but continues to accept them, based on the current workflow.'),('Assignment review and rating','Teachers provide a one-to-five rating and review comment. The student is notified. Reviewed work is locked against further replacement, preserving the assessed artifact. Rating-to-percentage conversion in performance charts is a display convention, not an exam score.'),('Study material upload and links','Teachers can share an uploaded file, a web link or both, with a title, subject and cohort. File validation and size rules apply. Students see materials for their department and semester, with filters for subject or type.'),('File access and lifecycle','The file endpoints check that a request comes from an authorized student, owning teacher or administrator. When a material or assignment is removed, uploaded files may be deleted if not referenced elsewhere. Backup and retention policy should include media storage.'),('Cross-feature consistency','Exams, assignments, material and attendance all depend on correct cohort data. A student transfer can change content visibility without rewriting historical records. Administrators should review downstream effects before changing department or semester.')]),
('12  Notifications, email and guardian communication','The notification subsystem provides in-app messages and records guardian email activity. Development and production behavior differ: when no SMTP host is configured, the console backend is used; real delivery requires environment configuration.',[
('In-app notification model','Notifications associate a recipient and optional sender with a type, title and message. They may link to related exams. Role-specific pages display notification counts and lists, giving users a centralized place for activity updates.'),('Exam publication notices','Publishing an exam creates messages for eligible students. The notice should communicate subject, title and opening/closing information. Recipient selection must use both department and semester to prevent unrelated students receiving the announcement.'),('Assignment and material notices','Creating assignments and sharing study material generates student notifications for the selected cohort. The teacher interface should show the target clearly before saving, and the server should derive recipients from the validated academic pair.'),('Attendance notices','Absent students can receive a notification that records date and cohort context. Guardian email is also attempted when contact details exist. Changes to attendance should follow a defined correction policy and must not create misleading duplicate messages.'),('Result email workflow','Exam completion may create a guardian email containing performance details. The email service logs sent or failed attempts. The portal should avoid including unnecessary sensitive detail in email and should respect institutional communication rules.'),('Email configuration','The settings read SMTP host, port, username, password, TLS and sender from environment variables. Without a host, Django uses the console email backend. Credentials should not be checked into source control; a test email should be sent before enabling production delivery.'),('Email failure handling','SMTP failures should not silently erase the academic event. EmailLog records outcome and diagnostic context. Administrators need a retry or follow-up procedure that avoids sending duplicate or outdated messages.'),('Guardian contact quality','Student records include guardian name and email fields. The administration process should verify contact details and document correction procedures. A valid-looking email address does not prove that the recipient is the authorized guardian.'),('Communication privacy and audit','Messages should disclose only information necessary for the intended recipient. Access to email logs should be restricted. Retention and export rules should be reviewed with college policy, especially where records include exam performance or attendance.')]),
('13  Verification and quality assurance','The repository includes an automated Django test module, but this document does not claim that the tests were run during report preparation. Verification should combine automated tests, manual role-based workflows, file and email checks, security review and representative user acceptance.',[
('Verification strategy','Verification asks whether the software follows its requirements; validation asks whether those requirements meet college needs. Use unit tests for helpers and forms, integration tests for workflow persistence, negative authorization tests for access boundaries, and acceptance tests with representative users.'),('Existing automated test coverage','The accounts test suite references authentication, department and semester behavior, exam and assignment features, student access and attendance. Its tests can reveal intended behavior but should be reviewed after the teacher department policy change so that outdated expectations are updated.'),('Functional test design','For every feature, test the valid path, invalid input, missing data, permission denial, repeated submission and boundary condition. Include department/semester combinations, time boundaries, file size and extension limits, review lock behavior and notification recipients.'),('Security test design','Attempt direct URL access across roles, access to another teacher’s exam, another student’s submission and protected files. Change query strings and hidden form values to verify server-side checks. Review CSRF coverage, session configuration, DEBUG mode and deployment headers.'),('Usability and accessibility review','Conduct keyboard-only navigation and screen-reader checks on login, exam, forms, status controls and modal confirmations. Evaluate error messages, focus behavior, contrast, responsive layout and timeout communication with representative students and staff.'),('Data integrity checks','Verify uniqueness constraints, cascading deletion, migrations, attendance uniqueness and consistency between profile academic scope and content records. Test backup restoration with both the database and media files.'),('Performance and reliability checks','Measure page latency, concurrent exam submissions, report queries and upload handling under a defined workload. The source does not provide service-level objectives. Establish a target environment and representative dataset before drawing capacity conclusions.'),('Defect management and evidence','Each defect should record reproducible steps, expected and actual outcome, severity, affected role and fix version. Capture redacted screenshots or logs. Test evidence should identify environment, browser, commit and date so an examiner can reproduce it.'),('Acceptance and sign-off','Acceptance criteria should be agreed with the guide or college stakeholder before demonstration. Record pass/fail status and open issues. Do not treat a successful local demo as evidence of production readiness or institutional approval.')]),
('14  Installation, deployment and maintenance','The repository README documents a local setup path using Python, a virtual environment, dependency installation, migrations, demo data and Django’s development server. Production deployment requires a separate hardened configuration and operational plan.',[
('Prerequisites','The project documentation lists Python 3.10 to 3.13 and VS Code as convenient development prerequisites. Django 5.2 is the web framework; Pillow supports image handling; scikit-learn supports text evaluation; OpenCV is optional for webcam checks.'),('Local setup on Windows','The documented Windows path runs setup.bat, selects the virtual environment interpreter and starts the server with run.bat or the VS Code launch configuration. The browser opens localhost. The server is stopped with Ctrl+C.'),('Manual setup and migrations','A manual setup creates a virtual environment, installs requirements, runs migrations, loads demo data and starts runserver. Migrations must be applied when schema changes are introduced. Existing database contents require deliberate backup and migration planning.'),('Demo data and accounts','The seed_demo command creates demonstration accounts and academic records. Demo credentials are for local demonstration only and should not be exposed in a live deployment. Reset mode deletes and recreates demo data, so it must not be used on real institutional records.'),('Static and media files','Static assets include CSS, JavaScript and bundled frontend libraries. Uploaded files and profile images are stored under media during development. Production should configure secure persistent storage and serve static assets through an appropriate server or object store.'),('Production hardening','Disable DEBUG, set a random secret key and explicit allowed hosts, configure HTTPS and secure cookies, run Django deployment checks, apply least-privilege database access and protect media. Remove demo accounts and rotate any default credentials.'),('Database operations','SQLite is configured by default. A production database choice should account for concurrent writes, backups, migrations and restore time. Schedule backups and periodically test restoration. Treat the database and uploaded files as a coordinated data set.'),('Logging and monitoring','Production should capture application errors, delivery failures, storage issues and service health without logging passwords or unnecessary student data. Monitoring should alert a named owner and document escalation steps.'),('Maintenance and upgrades','Review Django and dependency updates, run migrations in a staging copy, validate backup integrity and verify important workflows after deployment. Track release versions and keep a rollback plan. Upgrade scripts should report unmatched legacy department names for correction.')]),
('15  Limitations, future work and conclusion','SmartEdu demonstrates an integrated set of college workflows, but important institutional and technical decisions remain. The project’s most valuable next steps are policy alignment, stronger evaluation evidence, deployment hardening, accessibility review and integration planning.',[
('Current limitations','The written-answer evaluator relies on lexical similarity and keywords; browser proctoring can be bypassed and may produce false positives; SQLite is the default database; and the repository does not demonstrate single sign-on or college SIS integration. No formal performance, usability or scoring-accuracy measurements are supplied.'),('Fairness and assessment limitations','A single threshold or rubric may not fit every course. Automated scores require teacher review, particularly for written work and disputed outcomes. A future validation study should use educator-labeled responses, representative subjects, clear metrics and an appeal protocol.'),('Security and privacy work remaining','Production deployment requires secure settings, verified authorization, secure media storage, backups, monitoring and privacy governance. The college should define retention, consent or notice, guardian communication, breach response and user support procedures.'),('Future work: interoperability','Potential extensions include import/export formats, a student information system integration, calendar synchronization and single sign-on. Each integration needs a defined source of truth, data mapping, consent and error-recovery approach.'),('Future work: assessment quality','Rubric-based criteria, question banks, randomized forms and educator calibration could improve the assessment workflow. Any automated scoring expansion should be measured against independent teacher marks and include an accessible manual review process.'),('Future work: inclusive learning','Accessibility improvements, multilingual interface support, low-bandwidth behavior and alternative assessment arrangements can broaden participation. These enhancements should be co-designed and verified with students who rely on assistive technology.'),('Future work: analytics and governance','Longitudinal analytics could help educators identify support needs, but dashboards should avoid stigmatizing labels and unsupported predictions. Data minimization, explainability and role-based visibility should guide any predictive feature.'),('Project conclusion','SmartEdu provides a coherent foundation for managing exams, assignments, attendance, study material, performance feedback and communication in one Django application. Its modular apps and explicit academic scope support a traceable design. Responsible adoption depends on human oversight, institutional governance and verification beyond the local demonstration.'),('Final submission readiness','Before academic submission, replace placeholders, apply the college template, add authentic screenshots and diagrams, record actual testing evidence, update the table of contents, verify citations and obtain required signatures. The examiner should be able to trace each stated feature to a demonstration or source location.')])]

for ch, intro, topics in chapters:
    for idx,(title,body) in enumerate(topics[:8],1):
        # Page-specific traceability matrix keeps each chapter page actionable.
        matrix=[['Review lens','Project-specific observation'],['Purpose',title],['Evidence to verify',body.split('.')[0]+'.'],['Open academic input','Confirm policy, user acceptance and institution-specific expectations with the project guide.']]
        page(f'{ch}.{idx}  {title}', [intro, body], matrix, ch)

# 21 appendix pages, each is practical and individually scoped.
appendix_pages=[
('Appendix A  Administrator operating procedure','Use the administrator role to create teacher and student accounts, maintain academic records and review portal-wide information. Begin with verified source data and confirm the assigned department before saving a teacher.', [['Step','Action'],['1','Sign in with an administrator account.'],['2','Register or edit a user and validate unique identifiers.'],['3','Check department and semester values.'],['4','Review the saved record and account status.']]),
('Appendix B  Teacher operating procedure','A teacher should confirm the assigned department shown in the portal before preparing work. The department is fixed by the administrator; semester and other activity details are selected as applicable.', [['Activity','Checklist'],['Exam','Set schedule, add questions, preview, publish.'],['Assignment','Set topic and due date; review submissions.'],['Attendance','Choose date and semester; check cohort before save.'],['Material','Validate file or link and intended semester.']]),
('Appendix C  Student operating procedure','Students should sign in with their own credentials, check the dashboard for due work and use the academic portal for exams, assignments, resources, attendance and results. Screenshots or help requests should remove other students’ information.', [['Task','Student action'],['Exam','Read instructions and submit within the time window.'],['Assignment','Upload an allowed file before review.'],['Missed exam','Submit reason and proof if eligible.'],['Attendance','Review records and report discrepancies through college process.']]),
('Appendix D  Demonstration script','A reliable demonstration uses seeded records, a clean browser session and a planned sequence. Do not display passwords or real personal information. The guide should be told which flows are simulated and which depend on external SMTP or webcam configuration.', [['Sequence','Demonstration'],['1','Log in as admin and inspect teacher assignment.'],['2','Log in as teacher and create semester-specific activity.'],['3','Log in as student in matching cohort and verify visibility.'],['4','Show review, attendance, performance and notification records.']]),
('Appendix E  Acceptance test catalogue: accounts','These cases are candidate acceptance checks. Record actual result, environment and evidence during execution; expected outcomes are not proof that the test passed.', [['ID','Scenario','Expected outcome'],['ACC-01','Valid student registration','User and profile are saved.'],['ACC-02','Duplicate roll number','Validation prevents duplicate.'],['ACC-03','Invalid role access','Request is denied or redirected.'],['ACC-04','Teacher assigned department','Teacher workflow displays assigned scope.']]),
('Appendix F  Acceptance test catalogue: academic scope','Scope checks should include both permitted and denied combinations. Test manipulated query parameters and POST bodies to establish that server-side filtering is authoritative.', [['ID','Scenario','Expected outcome'],['SCP-01','Matching department and semester','Eligible content appears.'],['SCP-02','Wrong semester','Content is absent and inaccessible.'],['SCP-03','Teacher requests another department','Assigned department remains enforced.'],['SCP-04','MBA/MCA semester 5','Validation rejects the pair.']]),
('Appendix G  Acceptance test catalogue: exams','Exam acceptance testing covers the full lifecycle and boundary conditions. Configure test time windows carefully and use non-production accounts.', [['ID','Scenario','Expected outcome'],['EX-01','Draft without questions','Publication is blocked.'],['EX-02','Valid MCQ attempt','Answer is stored and scored by key.'],['EX-03','Closing time passed','New start is rejected.'],['EX-04','Teacher reviews mark','Updated result is saved and notified.']]),
('Appendix H  Acceptance test catalogue: assignments and uploads','Upload testing should include valid and invalid file types, size boundaries and ownership checks. Use harmless test files and clean them after testing.', [['ID','Scenario','Expected outcome'],['AS-01','Valid submission','File and note are stored.'],['AS-02','Disallowed extension','Form reports validation error.'],['AS-03','Oversize upload','Upload is rejected.'],['AS-04','Reviewed submission replacement','Change is blocked.']]),
('Appendix I  Acceptance test catalogue: attendance and email','Email tests should run against the console backend or a controlled test mailbox. Do not send real guardian messages during an unapproved demonstration.', [['ID','Scenario','Expected outcome'],['AT-01','Save present status','Attendance record is saved.'],['AT-02','Mark absent','Notification and email attempt are logged.'],['AT-03','Repeat same event','Duplicate guardian send is avoided.'],['AT-04','SMTP unavailable','Failure is recorded for administrator review.']]),
('Appendix J  Acceptance test catalogue: authorization and files','Authorization cases should use at least two teachers and two students. A direct URL check is important because hidden navigation is not an access control.', [['ID','Scenario','Expected outcome'],['SEC-01','Teacher opens another teacher exam','Access is denied.'],['SEC-02','Student opens another student file','Access is denied.'],['SEC-03','Authorized teacher opens proof','File is available.'],['SEC-04','Unauthenticated user opens protected route','Login is required.']]),
('Appendix K  Data dictionary: identity and profiles','The following summary is descriptive. Confirm field definitions against current migrations before schema changes or data imports.', [['Entity','Important fields','Purpose'],['User','username, role, phone, active','Identity and role.'],['StudentProfile','roll number, dept, semester, guardian','Academic and contact profile.'],['TeacherProfile','employee ID, subject, department','Teacher assignment.'],['Notification','recipient, type, title, message','In-app communication.']]),
('Appendix L  Data dictionary: academic work','Academic work records store cohort scope and ownership. This enables recipient selection and teacher ownership checks.', [['Entity','Important fields','Purpose'],['Exam','title, schedule, scope, creator, status','Assessment lifecycle.'],['Question','type, text, marks, topic, key','Exam item.'],['Assignment','topic, due date, scope, creator','Coursework.'],['StudyMaterial','title, file/link, scope, uploader','Learning resource.']]),
('Appendix M  Data dictionary: outcomes and operations','Outcome records contain educational data. Restrict access and follow retention policy when exporting or backing up these records.', [['Entity','Important fields','Purpose'],['Attendance','student, date, status, scope, marker','Presence record.'],['ExamSubmission','student, exam, timestamps, score','Attempt and outcome.'],['ReattemptRequest','reason, proof, status, deadline','Missed-exam review.'],['EmailLog','recipient, event, status, error','Delivery audit.']]),
('Appendix N  Route group reference','Root URL patterns include the following route groups. Consult app URL files for exact named routes and methods before documenting an API contract.', [['Prefix','Domain'],['/auth/','Authentication and account-related views'],['/admin-portal/','Administrative workflows'],['/teacher/','Teacher portal'],['/student/','Student portal'],['/exams/, /attendance/','Assessment and attendance services'],['/materials/, /assignments/','Resources and coursework']]),
('Appendix O  Configuration reference','Configuration values vary by environment. The local defaults are for development and should be reviewed before hosting.', [['Configuration','Purpose'],['SMARTEDU_SECRET_KEY','Django signing key; set a private random value.'],['SMARTEDU_DEBUG','Enable debug only locally.'],['SMARTEDU_ALLOWED_HOSTS','Allowed production host names.'],['SMARTEDU_EMAIL_*','SMTP connection and sender configuration.'],['AI_THRESHOLD_FAIL','Low-score flag threshold.']]),
('Appendix P  File and data backup checklist','Back up the database and uploaded media as a coordinated set. Keep encrypted copies in a separate protected location and periodically rehearse recovery.', [['Frequency','Action'],['Daily','Database and media backup according to policy.'],['Weekly','Check backup job status and storage capacity.'],['Monthly','Restore a copy in a safe environment.'],['After release','Verify migration and rollback plan.']]),
('Appendix Q  Academic project evidence checklist','The final report should be supported by authentic evidence, not assumed outcomes. Keep evidence redacted and label the environment used.', [['Evidence','Complete before submission'],['Design','Approved requirements and architecture diagram.'],['Implementation','Screenshots or code references for core workflows.'],['Testing','Executed cases with result, date and environment.'],['Review','Guide feedback and final correction record.']]),
('Appendix R  Glossary','This glossary defines terms as used in this report. Institutional policy definitions take precedence where they differ.', [['Term','Definition'],['Cohort','Students sharing a department and semester.'],['Draft exam','Assessment not yet published to students.'],['Re-attempt','An individually authorized opportunity after a missed or reset attempt.'],['Weak topic','A topic flagged by performance analysis for further review.']]),
('Appendix S  Known limitations register','Limitations should be tracked and re-evaluated with each release. The entries below reflect repository-level design constraints, not a claim that every deployment has the same impact.', [['Area','Limitation','Suggested action'],['Scoring','Lexical similarity can miss meaning.','Teacher review and benchmark study.'],['Proctoring','Browser checks are bypassable.','Treat logs as review signals.'],['Storage','SQLite is default.','Select and test production database.'],['Integration','No SIS/SSO shown.','Define integration scope.']]),
('Appendix T  Release checklist','Use this checklist for a controlled release candidate. Mark each item with the responsible person and date in the project’s submission copy.', [['Area','Check'],['Configuration','DEBUG off, secret and hosts set.'],['Data','Migrations and backups verified.'],['Security','Role and file access reviewed.'],['Workflows','Exam, assignment, attendance and email checked.'],['Documentation','Placeholders, contents and citations updated.']]),
('Appendix U  Student customization and final submission checklist','The project report must be personalized by its student author(s). Add only verified details and follow the college’s latest format.', [['Field','Student action'],['Identity','Enter name, roll number and programme.'],['Institution','Enter college, university and department.'],['Guide','Confirm guide name and designation.'],['Evidence','Insert authentic diagrams, screenshots and test results.'],['Approval','Collect required signatures and update contents.']]),
]
